Apple is apologizing after admitting a  software update deliberately slowed some  older iPhones the tech giant says the  update is intended to prevent sudden  shutdowns in the iPhone 6 but as a side  effect apps and other functions run  slower in a new message to customers the  company says quote we know that some of  you feel Apple has let you down we  apologize we have never and would never  do anything to intentionally shorten the  life of any Apple product Apple is  discounting the cost of battery  replacements on certain phones Dan  Ackerman is a section editor at our  partners CNET Dan great to have you back  with us at the table this is a bit  confusing Apple is apologizing but not  for what some of its users are accusing  it of doing so explain how this all went  down it's a complicated issue what Apple  did was come up with a not entirely  unreasonable solution to a problem  that's not very easy to fix and that's  the batteries that we use in everything  as they get older they don't hold as  much charge and they also can't give out  energy at the same peak levels and when  you have newer apps on phones that  really want to do impressive stuff they  need a lot of energy and people found  that on their older phones sometimes not  only was the battery life not as long  the phone would just shut down I found  that happened to me with my iPhone 6  plus so they had a software update  because so many things about these  devices are determined by by software  updates that come after you buy the  original device that said we're gonna  we're gonna smooth out some of those  peaks do some power management to  basically say the phone can't give out  as much energy to the apps as the apps  may sometimes ask for to make the  battery last longer so you don't have a  phone that lasts like three or four  hours you have one that'll last most of  the day you know it's interesting  because this did make me think when I  read this story when you used to have a  blackberry you can actually take the  battery out yourself and put a new one  in with the iPhone it's not easy to do  you'd actually have to go into the store  and have it done for you Apple is now  saying they're going to allow you to  follow your battery life more easier  easily on your iPhone explain that yes  they're not going to change what the  software update did in terms of doing  the power management and slowing down  your phone to extend the battery life  but they say that they're going to add  something to the software for it that  will let you monitor it and give you  more information more transparency  about what's happening and and that's  kind of the entire game here is  providing transparency that's one that  the polity does after the fact and  that's what I think people were more  upset about and then their older phones  slowing down a bit that the lack of  transparency that they weren't telling  you they were doing this to your product  you mentioned the apology how big of a  deal was that we heard sort of a little  apology and now a full apology that's  true an Apple over the years I've had a  handful of these situations where  they've done these kind of big apologies  the one that always comes to mind is  what they call antenna gate which was  actually way back 8010 where first they  said you're holding the phone wrong if  you can't get a call through and then  they said okay we'll give you a little  rubber bumper to go around and we're  sorry about that so it is rare but  apology to think are very important for  these big companies look what happened  with Samsung last year when they had  their own battery problems they  eventually came to a big apology a big  investigation shared a lot of  information with people and now people  are still buying Samsung the you know  galaxy and note phones that apology  really worked well for them you and I  have had conversations about there's a  lot of people have a sneaking suspicion  that the reason that these phones didn't  work is so that you would be forced to  upgrade to the newest model of an iPhone  is Apple addressing that they've denied  that by the way I think what they were  trying to do at least what they say they  were trying to do is the opposite make  your phone feel like it'll last longer  because the battery doesn't run out as  quickly and you don't feel like you have  to get a new phone but by making the  phones feel more sluggish they actually  ended up maybe doing the opposite I had  an iPhone six-plus that was really  getting kind of pokey and I upgraded to  a 10 this year when I gave my six to  somebody I work with the first thing  they did was go oh man you've got to get  a new phone really slow to them it's  rare you get an apology from Apple and  while they did offer an apology they  also explained in their apologies with  batteries in laughs yeah yeah and it's  true and this batteries and everything  we own not just phones it is the  sluggishness that leads to all of us to  new purchases Dan Ackerman thank you  very much okay
 

